s  of covet-19 in canada  in its annual report the agency says the  pandemic has had a profound  impact on canadians well-being people  have reported  exercising less and drinking more the  opioid crisis is escalating and mental  health issues  are on the rise all canadians are  feeling the impact but it's been  hardest on seniors essential workers  racialized canadians  women and people with disabilities dr  theresa tam is calling for  structural change to address  inequalities  the virus didn't create new inequities  in our society it  exposed them these findings are more  than just uncomfortable facts about our  country during this pandemic  they are the lived realities of  countless canadians  in terms of canada's financial health  we're getting a better sense of how long  it might take to recover from covet 19.  the bank of canada is keeping its  benchmark interest rate at 0.25 percent  and it plans to keep it there until 2023  for more on the forecast and the factors  at play let's bring in david aiken in  ottawa  david what is the government banking on  to get the economy back on track  well robin if you wanted to buy that new  car or do some home renovations or maybe  borrow a bit more to get yourself  through the next few months go ahead the  bank of canada has got your back  if you're a household considering making  a major purchase  if you're a business considering  investing  you can be confident that interest rates  will be low for a long time  once upon a time bank of canada  governors worried about ballooning  household debt  no longer this one tiff macklin wants  canadians to spend  and that's why the bank took the  extraordinary step wednesday of all but  guaranteeing that interest rates will  remain as low as possible  until 2023 we do think it's important  to be as clear as we can about how we  see things today  that will help canadians make the  decisions that they need to make  consumer spending was the key to the  recent reopening recovery and 