ensive  care beds are still available  the rise in infections has prompted a  new partial lockdown  beginning november 2nd restaurants and  bars will close except for takeout  live theater and movie theaters will  shut there will be no crowds at sports  events and those who can work from home  are being encouraged to do so up next  crafting change how a nigerian man's  work is increasing  inclusivity  every year thousands of canadians lose a  limb  the experience is traumatic and has a  profound impact on a person's life  an artificial limb is usually a major  part of the recovery process  but for someone who isn't white it can  be hard and expensive to find one that  matches their skin tone  crystal commansing has the story of a  nigerian man who is trying to make the  industry  more inclusive hands breasts ears  any body part needed john sande is ready  to create it  he's an industrial fine artist but his  work took him in a new direction  about three years ago when his brother  lost fingers in an  accident his option for a prosthesis  was a white hat this behavior changed  know this guy is not happy  and god forbid he could have taken his  life maybe  so i had to do something fast and quick  the global prosthetics industry  is expanding to include more skin tones  but it's a relatively  small industry product availability is  based on demand and the cost of  manufacturing  on volume the war amps says a non-white  amputee  can end up spending more for a  prosthesis there aren't any  sort of off-the-shelf options but what  the company can do  is paint the color or or do a customized  version but then that can get very  expensive annalise petlock says  health coverage for canadians depends on  what province you live in  war amps through its key tag donation  program helps cover  some of the costs for children and  adults but  many people still end up having to  fundraise  a prosthetic leg that starts above the  knee for example  can cost sixty thousand dollars you  